@using Odyssey.Dtos.Finance

@*
    OdsContractPartyRoleSelect — a single-select pre-wired to the ContractPartyRole vocabulary
    (Odyssey Design System · components/ContractPartyRoleSelect, issue #121): what a linked record
    DOES in the agreement, each member rendered with its Material icon in its category color.

    A thin, domain-typed wrapper over the shared OdsTypeSelect — every prop passes straight through
    and this adds no behaviour, markup or state of its own, which is what lets the role field inherit
    OdsTypeSelect's accessible name, listbox semantics, keyboard model and error association rather
    than re-implementing any of them (#122 §6.1). Value is the enum key. Bind via @bind-Value.

    A plain list rather than a card grid: fifteen members is a list, and the role is the one field a
    reader scans for.

    Since issue #157 it is the ONLY picker in the system whose option list depends on ANOTHER field.
    Pass `ContractType` and the control offers exactly the roles that type accepts — suggested ones
    first, under their own group heading — read off the SHARED `ContractPartyRoleMatrix` in
    Odyssey.Dtos, the same declaration the server's write-path validator consults. So the picker
    cannot offer a role the API would answer with a 422, and the 422 becomes a backstop rather than
    something the user is walked into. Omit `ContractType` and the full vocabulary is offered
    ungrouped, which is what a surface with no contract in hand gets.

    There is no default and no empty member: `Unspecified` was retired with the matrix, so the control
    starts on its placeholder and the form's save stays disabled until a role is picked.
*@

<OdsTypeSelect Value="@Value"
               ValueChanged="ValueChanged"
               Types="Options"
               Groups="Groups"
               Id="@Id"
               Label="@Label"
               Placeholder="@Placeholder"
               Help="@Help"
               Error="@Error"
               Required="@Required"
               Disabled="@Disabled"
               Class="@Class"
               @attributes="UserAttributes" />

@code {
    /// <summary>Selected enum key. Bindable via @bind-Value.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Value { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The contract's type, which decides which roles are legal. Null offers the whole vocabulary
    /// ungrouped — for a surface that has no contract in hand, never as a way to bypass the matrix.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public ContractType? ContractType { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string> ValueChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Element id for the control, so a label or a describedby can point at it.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Id { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string? Label { get; set; } = "Role";

    [Parameter] public string? Placeholder { get; set; } = "Select role…";

    [Parameter] public string? Help { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string? Error { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public bool Required { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public bool Disabled { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public Dictionary<string, object>? UserAttributes { get; set; }

    private IReadOnlyList<OdsTypeSelectGroup>? Groups =>
        ContractType is { } type ? OdsTypeRegistries.ContractPartyRolesFor(type) : null;

    // OdsTypeSelect renders Groups when present and Types otherwise; the flattened list is still
    // passed so the selected option resolves its own label and glyph either way.
    private IReadOnlyList<OdsTypeOption> Options =>
        Groups is { } groups ? [.. groups.SelectMany(group => group.Items)] : OdsTypeRegistries.ContractPartyRoles;
}
